{"name":"Yellow Green","hex":"#c8fd3d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yellow-green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A yellowish-green colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["A yellowish-green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow-green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chartreuse (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Chartreuse is a color between yellow and green also referred to as yellow-green or greenish yellow. It was named because of its resemblance to the French liqueur green chartreuse, introduced in 1764. Similarly, chartreuse yellow is a yellow color mixed with a small amount of green, named after the drink yellow chartreuse. The wavelength and frequency of chartreuse is near 547.5 nm or 547.5 THz."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/RAF-Cardington-Shed02_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-RAF-Cardington-Shed02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chartreuse_(color)","wikidata":"Q1153119"},{"title":"Shades of chartreuse","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of chartreuse are listed below. Chartreuse is a color between yellow and green, so named because of its resemblance to the color of the French liqueur green chartreuse."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Shades_of_chartreuse_%28x25%29.svg/330px-Shades_of_chartreuse_%28x25%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_chartreuse","wikidata":"Q105563495"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow","wikidata":"Q943"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Catopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG/500px-Catopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Catopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG/1280px-Catopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Catopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG/500px-Catopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACatopsilia_pomona_by_kadavoor.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Henry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg/500px-Henry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Henry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg/1280px-Henry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Henry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg/500px-Henry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenry_Prince_of_Wales_on_the_Hunting_Field_Robert_Peake.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/10_Jahre_SRZ_-_Schutz_%26_Rettung_Z%C3%BCrich_-_%27Parade%27_-_Feuerwehr_K%C3%BCsnacht_2011-05-13_20-37-44.jpg/500px-10_Jahre_SRZ_-_Schutz_%26_Rettung_Z%C3%BCrich_-_%27Parade%27_-_Feuerwehr_K%C3%BCsnacht_2011-05-13_20-37-44.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/10_Jahre_SRZ_-_Schutz_%26_Rettung_Z%C3%BCrich_-_%27Parade%27_-_Feue